Article 57
The cluster of children's rights as referred to in Article 56 paragraph (4) letter b which includes:
a. Civil rights and freedoms which include:
1) the right of the child to identity;
2) the right to protection of identity;
3) the right to express and voice opinions;
4) the right to think, to conscience, to religion, and to belief;
5) the right to organize and assemble peacefully;
6) the right to protection of private life;
7) the right to access appropriate information; and
8) the right to be free from torture and other cruel, inhuman, or degrading punishment or treatment.
b. The right to family environment and care which includes:
1) parental guidance and responsibility;
2) children separated from parents;
3) reunification;
4) illegal transfer of children;
5) welfare support;
6) children who are forced to be separated from the family environment;
7) child adoption;
8) periodic review of placement; and
9) violence and neglect.
c. The right of the child to basic health and welfare which includes:
a. children with disabilities obtaining access to health services and their welfare;
b. health and health services;
c. social security health services and facilities; and
d. standard of living;
d. The right of the child to education, leisure time utilization and cultural activities which includes:
1) education;
2) the objectives of education; and
3) holiday activities, cultural activities, and sports.
e. Protection for children in special protection which includes:
1) children in emergency situations;
2) children in conflict with the law;
3) children in situations of exploitation; and
4) children who are part of minority and isolated groups.

Part Four
Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level Article 48 (1) The Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in Article 45 letter c, constitutes a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Sub-District level.
(2) The Management of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. elements of the Sub-District apparatus;
b. agencies or institutions at the Sub-District level;
c. elements of the Sector Police;
d. elements of the Military District Command;
e. community leaders or religious leaders;
f. youth elements; and
g. elements of the Sub-District Family Welfare Development Mobilization Team. (3) The main duties of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protection of women and children;
b. receiving public complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Sub-District and Village areas;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance for victims or referring to P2TP2A, sector police/resort police, hospitals or other institutions as required.
(4) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the task force for the protection of women and children at the sub-district level as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Decree of the Sub-District Head.
